---
import StandardsLayout from "../../components/StandardsLayout.astro";
import CitationBlock from "../../components/CitationBlock.astro";
import SectionBlock from "../../components/SectionBlock.astro";
import { derivePublicationMetadata } from "../../utils/publication";

const pageTitle = "EU AI Act";
const pageDescription =
  "An Ethotechnics critique of the EU AI Act: most high-risk systems are self-assessed, their authority does not lapse, and a challenge need not reach them.";
const permalink = "/standards/eu-ai-act";
const publishedTime = "2026-02-15T00:00:00Z";
const modifiedTime = "2026-10-06T00:00:00Z";
const timelineReviewed = "2026-05-20";
const critiqueRevised = "2026-10-01";
const anchorLinks = [
  { href: "#overview", label: "What it is" },
  { href: "#critique", label: "Ethotechnics critique" },
  { href: "#failure-mode", label: "Failure mode" },
  { href: "#implementation-cluster", label: "Implementation controls path" },
  { href: "#timeline", label: "Timeline status" },
  { href: "#implications-now", label: "Implications now" },
  { href: "#keep-current", label: "Sources and updates" },
];
const publication = derivePublicationMetadata({
  permalink,
  published: publishedTime,
  updated: modifiedTime,
  changelogSummary:
    "Removed an internal drafting note, a review schedule the page did not keep, and a review pitch from the implementation path.",
});
---

<StandardsLayout
  title={pageTitle}
  description={pageDescription}
  permalink={permalink}
  seoTitle="EU AI Act: critique and implementation controls — Standards"
  eyebrow="Regulatory regime"
  anchorLinks={anchorLinks}
  publishedTime={publishedTime}
  modifiedTime={modifiedTime}
>
  <SectionBlock
    id="overview"
    eyebrow="Overview"
    title="What it is"
    description="A legal regime with risk-tiering, conformity assessment, and market surveillance."
  >
    <p>
      The EU AI Act establishes obligations for high-risk systems, requires
      human oversight, and enforces compliance through EU and national
      authorities.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Resource status:</strong> critique revised {critiqueRevised};
      timeline status reviewed {timelineReviewed}.
    </p>
  </SectionBlock>

  <SectionBlock
    id="critique"
    eyebrow="Ethotechnics critique"
    title="Where the regime falls short"
    description="The Act requires assessment before release and monitoring after it (Art. 72). The gaps are in what the running system owes the people it decides about."
    variant="alt"
  >
    <ul class="card__list">
      <li>
        <strong>Self-assessed authority does not expire.</strong> Most high-risk systems
        listed in Annex III are assessed by their own provider before release (Art.
        43(2)), and assessed again only after a substantial modification (Art. 43(4)).
        A notified body's certificate lapses after at most four or five years (Art.
        44(2)); a provider's own assessment does not, and nothing requires it to be
        renewed on evidence.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>A challenge does not have to reach the system.</strong> A person affected
        by a high-risk system's decision can ask the deployer to explain it (Art.
        86), and anyone can complain to a market surveillance authority that the Act
        was breached (Art. 85). The Act gives no route to contest the decision itself;
        that comes from other law, such as GDPR Article 22. No provision in either
        ties a contest that succeeds to a change in the system.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong
          >Cost to people is assessed before use, not counted after.</strong
        >
        Public bodies, private entities providing public services, and deployers that
        score credit or price life and health insurance assess the impact on fundamental
        rights before first use, and update the assessment when its elements change
        (Art. 27). Nothing requires a measure of what the running system costs the
        people it decides about, such as the time they spend correcting its errors.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>A halt is required; rehearsing it is not.</strong> Human oversight
        must, as appropriate and proportionate, let a person interrupt a high-risk
        system so it halts in a safe state (Art. 14(4)(e)). Nothing requires anyone
        to rehearse the halt or meet a recovery deadline.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </SectionBlock>

  <SectionBlock
    id="failure-mode"
    eyebrow="Failure mode"
    title="Lawful deployment + harmful latency"
    description="Compliance can coexist with prolonged pending states and slow restoration."
  >
    <p>
      The failure mode is a system that is compliant yet still traps people in
      extended “pending” states without enforceable restoration timelines.
    </p>
  </SectionBlock>

  <SectionBlock
    id="implementation-cluster"
    eyebrow="Implementation controls"
    title="From the requirement to the controls"
    description="Two steps in order: understand the requirement, then map it to controls."
    variant="alt"
  >
    <ol class="muted">
      <li>
        <strong>Explainer:</strong>
        <a href="#timeline">the timeline status</a> on this page, for what is in force
        and what is scheduled.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Mechanism template:</strong>
        <a href="/standards/enforceable-governance-crosswalks"
          >Control crosswalk workbook</a
        >
        to map obligations to accountable controls.
      </li>
    </ol>
  </SectionBlock>

  <SectionBlock
    id="timeline"
    eyebrow="Timeline status"
    title="Direction is stable; dates can still move"
  >
    <p>
      We treat the Commission timeline as the canonical baseline: entry into
      force, phased obligations, and later applicability windows for some
      categories.
    </p>
    <p>
      We also track live policy updates (including proposals that could shift
      parts of implementation timing). This means schedule details are
      politically live, even while the governance direction remains consistent.
    </p>
  </SectionBlock>

  <SectionBlock
    id="implications-now"
    eyebrow="Implications"
    title="What organizations should optimize for right now"
    description="Prepare for converging governance expectations, not a single date bet."
    variant="alt"
  >
    <ul class="card__list">
      <li>
        <strong>Prioritize auditability primitives:</strong> receipts, decision records,
        evidence packs, and repair logs that survive policy edits.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Operationalize time bounds:</strong> define and test time-to-human,
        time-to-remedy, and escalation paths with named authority.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Design for contestability:</strong> include standing, non-retaliation,
        and reliable recourse pathways that can be verified in an audit.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Crosswalk controls:</strong> map one control library across EU AI
        Act, NIST AI RMF, and ISO/IEC 42001 so readiness does not hinge on one regime.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </SectionBlock>

  <SectionBlock
    id="keep-current"
    eyebrow="Maintenance"
    title="Sources and updates"
  >
    <ul class="card__list">
      <li>
        <strong>Sources:</strong> Commission primary materials first, then reputable
        reporting where a proposal or a date is still contested.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Updates:</strong> the page changes when a timeline, an obligation's
        scope, or an enforcement mechanism materially changes. The dates it was last
        revised are at the top of the page.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </SectionBlock>
  <CitationBlock
    title={pageTitle}
    permalink={permalink}
    publication={publication}
  />
</StandardsLayout>
